1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 10: receiving antennas: Beverage and EWE

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:16,071
YA-NEC course, lesson 10: receiving antennas - Beverage and EWE. Calculators, Receiving antennas family.

3
00:00:16,121 --> 00:00:29,506
Beverage (Harold Beverage, 1920s) for 160 m: 175 m of wire, 1.07 wavelengths, 2.5 m high, terminated with 500 ohm to a 20 ohm ground stake.

4
00:00:29,556 --> 00:00:41,342
The calculator shows the length for best response at 20 degrees and the wire impedance over ground: about 524 ohm, so a 9:1 transformer to 50-75 ohm.

5
00:00:41,392 --> 00:01:17,754
'Create the NEC model and compute the RDF': wires, down-leads, load and source, with the Sommerfeld ground.

6
00:01:17,804 --> 00:01:28,129
The model opens: a long wire along X, lobe toward the termination end.

7
00:01:28,179 --> 00:01:42,801
Back to the tab, the receiving figures: RDF 10.6 dB (IV3PRK measured 10.81), DMF 11.5 dB, F/B 23 dB, 5.5 dB better S/N than a vertical.

8
00:01:42,851 --> 00:01:51,669
The absolute gain is -11.4 dBi: it does not matter for reception, as long as a 12 dB preamplifier makes up for it.

9
00:01:51,719 --> 00:02:12,027
'Optimize for F/B' searches the termination resistor.

10
00:02:12,077 --> 00:02:21,688
400 ohm: F/B from 23 to 38.5 dB, RDF 10.7 dB. The rear null is set by the termination.

11
00:02:21,738 --> 00:02:28,987
Now a small one: the EWE by Floyd Koontz WA2WVL (QST, February 1995).

12
00:02:29,037 --> 00:02:36,851
Two 3 m verticals and 9.1 m of wire - 0.06 wavelengths - with about 850 ohm termination.

13
00:02:36,901 --> 00:03:15,545
'Create the NEC model and compute the RDF'.

14
00:03:15,595 --> 00:03:30,559
RDF 7.7 dB, F/B 31.7 dB, impedance 847 - j536 ohm through a 16:1 transformer. In a garden instead of 175 m of field.

15
00:03:30,609 --> 00:03:36,184
Gain -28 dBi: here a 20 dB preamplifier is needed.

16
00:03:36,234 --> 00:04:06,346
'Optimize for F/B' on the EWE too.

17
00:04:06,396 --> 00:04:12,479
800 ohm: F/B 34.2 dB, RDF 7.8 dB.

18
00:04:12,529 --> 00:04:21,289
Below, the RDF ranking for 160 m: measured antennas (IV3PRK, Hi-Z) and, in orange, what YA-NEC just computed.

19
00:04:21,339 --> 00:04:31,756
The long Beverages lead the ranking; the EWE sits next to the K9AY and the Flags. RDF compares receiving antennas fairly, whatever their gain.

20
00:04:31,806 --> 00:04:42,639
Beverage and EWE: long or small, the RDF tells which one hears better. Next lesson: Four-square and the other receiving antennas. 73 de IC8TEM
